  "preamble": "Devoicing of the voiced bilabial stop [b] to tenuis [p] is a common unconditioned or initial-position change. It occurred across most Xiang and southern Bingzhou Jin varieties of Chinese, where Middle Chinese voiced initials became unaspirated voiceless stops; in Oghuric Turkic (Chuvash pin 'thousand' vs Turkish bin); in Hmongic, where the lost voicing survives as a low tone register; in Kott and Arin among the Yeniseian languages; and in Chamorro, where p is the regular reflex of Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *b.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Devoicing, Voice Loss, Register Split",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Xiang Chinese (Changsha)",
      "languageFamily": "Sinitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "白 MC baek [b]",
          "to": "Changsha pɤ (tone 7)",
          "note": "Voiced initials give unaspirated voiceless initials in all tones in most Xiang varieties; Chengbu keeps ba7."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Jin Chinese (Bingzhou)",
      "languageFamily": "Sinitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "[b]",
          "to": "[p]",
          "note": "Voiced obstruents of Middle Chinese become tenuis in all four tones."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Chuvash",
      "languageFamily": "Turkic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*bïŋ \"1000\" [b]",
          "to": "пин (pin) [pin]",
          "note": "Oghuric initial *b- > p-; Turkish bin keeps b-."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Proto-Hmong-Mien to White Hmong (Hmong Daw)",
